=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r3
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** r3.eth0 — the physical interface on collision domain J that provides the only direct link between r3 and r9.

**Mechanism:** r3.eth0 is administratively/operationally DOWN (`<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` flags only, no `UP,LOWER_UP`). With this link down, r3 cannot exchange OSPF Hellos with r9, so the OSPF adjacency between them collapses. Once the adjacency is gone, r9's OSPF-advertised prefixes — 19.0.0.0/8 (r9 loopback), 39.0.0.0/8 (r3–r9 link subnet), and 90.0.0.0/8 (r9.eth1) — are flushed from r3's LSDB and therefore disappear from r1's RIB, producing the "unstable route" symptom observed by the team.

**Localization:** r3 / eth0 (segment J, 39.1.1.1/8 side). Carrier-changes = 2 (one bring-up at boot, one bring-down after start-up) with no dmesg link events, confirming a static `link_down` rather than a flap.

**R9 is healthy:** Direct shell access to r9 confirms eth0 (39.1.1.2/8) and eth1 (90.1.1.1/8) are both UP with correct IPs and routes intact. R9 did not hang or crash during the maintenance window; the team's reboot request is based on incorrect attribution.

**Root cause:** r3.eth0 was brought DOWN (likely as part of the maintenance activity), severing the r3–r9 OSPF link and withdrawing R9's prefix advertisements from the entire routing domain.

**Fix:** On r3, run `ip link set eth0 up` to restore the interface; the OSPF adjacency to r9 will re-establish and the three affected prefixes will re-appear in r1's RIB within one OSPF Hello interval.
=== END ===